## Why This Is Asked

Explore on-device continual learning for personalization with strict privacy, covering hardware costs, OTA bandwidth, drift, and safety.

## Key Concepts

- On-device continual learning and adapters
- Delta patching and quantized updates
- Privacy-by-design and data minimization
- Drift detection and rollback/fault tolerance
- OTA rollout planning and monitoring

```javascript
// Pseudo delta apply for adapter update
function applyDeltaAdapter(model, delta) {
  for (let i = 0; i < delta.length; i++) {
    model.adapter[i] += delta[i];
  }
  return model;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure break-even horizon under varying device churn and OTA costs?
- What privacy controls would you implement to prevent leakage through delta metadata?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design privacy-preserving retention controls at the device edge, balancing storage, CPU, and regulatory costs while quantifying the business impact of data minimization.

## Key Concepts

- On-device policy engines and interpreters
- Data minimization and retention windows
- Batch policy delivery and anonymized audit deltas
- Compliance costs and telemetry design

## Code Example

```javascript
// Policy evaluator
function shouldDelete(event, policy) {
  const rule = policy.findRule(event.type);
  return event.timestamp < Date.now() - policy.getRetentionPeriod(rule);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a practical on-device data structure with incremental updates, offline-first UX, and a clear 12‑month cost plan, including privacy considerations.

## Key Concepts

- Local inverted index and incremental updates
- Offline-first data paths and cache invalidation
- Delta-based cloud sync and conflict resolution
- Resource budgeting: storage, CPU, memory, latency, energy
- Privacy, compliance, and user consent handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal incremental inverted index (conceptual)
class InvertedIndex {
  constructor() {
    this.index = {}; // term -> Set of note IDs
  }
  addNote(id, text) {
    text.split(/\W+/).forEach(t => {
      if (!t) return;
      const w = t.toLowerCase();
      (this.index[w] ||= new Set()).add(id);
    });
  }
  search(q) {
    return q
      .toLowerCase()
      .split(/\s+/)
      .flatMap(w => Array.from(this.index[w] || []));
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you estimate index maintenance energy and storage growth over time?
- How would you handle search latency guarantees on mid-range devices with thousands of notes?
- What privacy safeguards would you implement for on-device indexing and sync deltas?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess understanding of privacy-preserving, on-device analytics and the viability of data-sharing via aggregates.

## Key Concepts

- On-device analytics and data minimization
- User consent and opt-in UX
- Aggregated data leakage risk and privacy controls
- Cost modeling: device runtime, memory, and backend bandwidth
- Rollout strategy, drift monitoring, and governance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple rolling aggregate for 90-day window on-device
function rollingAvg(values, window=90) {
  let sum=0;
  const res=[];
  for(let i=0;i<values.length;i++){
    sum+=values[i];
    if(i>=window) sum-=values[i-window];
    if(i>=window-1) res.push(sum/(window));
  }
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate accuracy of aggregates vs server baselines?
- What are rollback or revocation strategies if consent changes mid-rollout?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to scope CBAs for privacy-preserving on-device ML and quantify energy, storage, bandwidth, and compliance trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Federated learning
- Gradient compression
- Privacy/compliance overhead
- Cost modeling
- Rollout strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeCBA(params){
  const {devices, energyPerDevice, uplink, cloud, retention} = params;
  const energy = devices * energyPerDevice;
  const total = energy + uplink + cloud + retention;
  const breakeven = total / Math.max(1, (params.revenuePerUser || 1));
  return {energy, total, breakeven};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle drift and model staleness in rollout?
- What metrics would you track to decide go/no-go, and how would you test them in a pilot?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to model costs for on-device features with privacy constraints, focusing on storage, compute, telemetry, and go/no-go criteria. It emphasizes practical trade-offs and measurable outcomes.

## Key Concepts
- On-device compute budgeting
- Data minimization and privacy compliance
- Telemetry design and cost optimization
- Index sizing and update cadence
- Break-even analysis and milestone-driven go/no-go criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeCBA(params) {
  const storageCost = params.indexMB * 0.15;
  const energyCost = params.annualWh * 0.0003;
  const telemetryCost = params.dailyTelemetryKB * 365 * 0.00001;
  const privacyAuditCost = params.userCount * 0.10;

  return {
    storageCost,
    energyCost,
    telemetryCost,
    privacyAuditCost,
    totalYear1Cost: storageCost + energyCost + telemetryCost + privacyAuditCost
  };
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to translate feature ideas into a privacy-conscious cost-benefit analysis with concrete metrics, edge/cloud trade-offs, and rollout risks.

## Key Concepts
- Local aggregation, data minimization, privacy-by-design
- Edge/cloud trade-offs, offline fallback, consent UX
- Rollout sequencing, drift detection, break-even analysis

